WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.430
Mexican director Guillermo del Toro is one
of the most imaginative and visionary filmmakers

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:06.650
of our time.

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:10.200
Throughout his acclaimed career, del Toro's
obsession with movie monsters has captured

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.660
the hearts and minds of both critics and audiences
worldwide.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:18.550
Each of del Toro's films are instantly recognizable,
as few directors can match the intricacy of

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:19.970
his distinctive vision.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:24.980
After twenty five years of moviemaking without
an Academy Award, 2018's The Shape of Water

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.450
won del Toro the Oscar for Best Picture and
Best Director.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:32.960
However, long before he dove into the depths
of inter-species love, del Toro tackled a

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.330
whole range of creature creations transcending
typical notions of horror and fantasy.

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:41.829
Join us as we rank the maestro Guillermo del
Toro's ten movies from worst to best.

00:00:41.829 --> 00:00:46.239
"When you made Shape of Water did people say,
"'why didn't you make it in spanish?

00:00:46.239 --> 00:00:47.950
Why did you make it in English?'"

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:51.050
"It was weird enough in any language."

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.050
Mimic

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:57.000
Anyone who's squeamish at the sight of bugs
would do best to avoid del Toro's first English-language

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.899
movie, 1997's Mimic, in which entomologist
Susan Tyler is on a quest to rid New York

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:04.460
City of deadly, disease-spreading cockroaches.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:09.150
Fighting fire with fire, Tyler genetically
engineers new insects called the Judas Breed

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.159
to feed on the infected bugs.

00:01:11.159 --> 00:01:15.440
Unexpectedly, or perhaps expectedly if you've
ever seen a horror movie, the new creatures

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.439
evolve in dangerous ways and take on a purpose
of their own.

00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:23.000
While Mimic features del Toro's renowned detailed
character designs, his first Hollywood venture

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.120
was ultimately a bitter experience.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:30.850
Creatively, he felt stifled by studio interference
and has since disowned Mimic entirely.

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:32.100
Crimson Peak

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.840
What do you get when you cross a haunted house
movie and a gothic love story?

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:41.240
For his ninth film, del Toro blurred the usual
boundaries of genre with 2015's Crimson Peak.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.159
This flick follows the dark love affair between
American heiress Edith Cushing and English

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:46.500
baronet Thomas Sharpe.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:51.210
Swept away to Sharpe's creepy yet magnificent
house, called Crimson Peak, the couple encounters

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:55.840
imaginative red ghosts and the potential threat
Thomas' sister Lucille brings.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.750
Marketed as an outright horror movie, reception
to Crimson Peak was mixed, scoring a 71 percent

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:05.610
on Rotten Tomatoes thanks largely to its stunning
set design and unnerving visuals.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.471
Without the scares of Pan's Labyrinth, nor
the romanticism in The Shape of Water, this

00:02:09.471 --> 00:02:14.280
gothic tale still sports del Toro's typical
style and sophistication.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.720
Pacific Rim

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.099
del Toro's biggest money-making blockbuster,
Pacific Rim, combines his love of Japanese

00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:22.230
mecha and menacing movie monsters.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:26.330
The story is a battle between destructive
creatures called Kaiju and the Earth's final

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:29.960
savior, gigantic robots called Jaegers piloted
by humans.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:34.380
Close to defeat, mankind's last resort is
to enlist the help of a washed-up ex-pilot

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:37.060
and a young trainee to stop the planet's impending
doom.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:40.950
Flush with heart-pounding action sequences,
Pacific Rim is not another mindless summer

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:41.950
smash up.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.990
Besides being a technical marvel, the characters
themselves are rich in diversity and depth.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:50.430
In del Toro's hands, these giant sea creatures
and massive robots are packed with more personality

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:51.930
than your standard monster flick.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:57.760
"I didn't want them to look like a certain
mecha or look like a certain piece of anime

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.560
or a another movie, I wanted them to be all
their own."

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:02.830
Cronos

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:08.019
Del Toro's first feature, 1994's Cronos, delivered
one of his most outlandish concepts for a

00:03:08.019 --> 00:03:13.260
vampire film: An elderly antique dealer discovers
an ancient artifact meant to grant eternal

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:14.260
life.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.569
With his eight-year-old granddaughter, he
tries to use the item's power for himself,

00:03:17.569 --> 00:03:19.940
but of course, things don't go as expected.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:24.150
Originally written for veteran actor Max Von
Sydow, the lead role is unusual as older men

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.210
rarely take center stage in film.

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:30.530
While sometimes struggling to maintain a balance
between horror and comedy, Cronos still showed

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:35.390
the del Toro horror magic he would capitalize
on later in his career.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:36.420
Hellboy

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:41.570
Well before superhero movies dominated theatres,
in 2004 Guillermo del Toro struggled to bring

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:45.090
his passion project, the comic book character
Hellboy, to cinema.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:49.640
Played by longtime friend Ron Perlman, Hellboy
is a surly, wise-cracking demon groomed to

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.320
be part of an elite paranormal secret defense
team.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.349
Based on writer and illustrator Mike Mignola's
acclaimed comic book series, Hellboy was a

00:03:56.349 --> 00:03:59.080
breath of fresh air in a world of vanilla
superheroes.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:03.870
Del Toro fought to retain the spirit of the
source material, despite studio interference.

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:07.640
Hellboy's humor and uniqueness prevailed,
and del Toro would expand Hellboy's universe

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.060
in the film's sequel.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:11.209
Blade II

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:15.730
Four years after actor Wesley Snipes brought
vampire hunter Blade to the big screen, the

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:21.109
supernatural Marvel hero returned for a 2002
sequel with del Toro in the director's chair.

00:04:21.109 --> 00:04:25.590
In Blade 2, Blade is pitted against a new
threat of mutated, savage vampires known as

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:26.590
the Reapers.

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:31.020
To defeat them, he agrees to an unexpected
truce, and reunites with his former mentor.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:35.410
A second round of Blade success was tricky
as del Toro had no involvement with the franchise

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:36.410
previously.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:40.949
Fortunately, he used his experience on Cronos
to expand the vampire mythology even further

00:04:40.949 --> 00:04:44.010
diving headfirst into Blade's hedonistic underworld.

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:48.630
Like Pacific Rim, Blade II lacks the finesse
of Del Toro's smaller movies, but his underrated

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:50.970
eye for action works exceptionally well here.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:56.270
"I wanted the violence to be balletic, to
be almost like a musical number, so it would

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:59.030
not have the yuck factor."

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:01.000
Hellboy II: The Golden Army

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:05.831
The only sequel to one of his own films he
actually returned to direct, in 2008 del Toro

00:05:05.831 --> 00:05:08.270
returned for Hellboy II: The Golden Army.

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:12.260
In the sequel, our hero is caught in a power
struggle between humanity and the invisible

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:16.370
realm as Prince Nuada breaks an ancient truce
to wage war on our world.

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:20.660
With the help of his friends, Hellboy steps
up to save Earth, but with conflict more complicated

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:22.090
than it first appears.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:26.070
The Golden Army is clearly the film that del
Toro wanted to make all along.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:29.860
With the most lovable assortment of oddballs
to hit the screen since the X-Men made their

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.259
debut, the movie dazzles in both innovative
action sequences and the character's hilarious

00:05:34.259 --> 00:05:35.639
moments of down time.

00:05:35.639 --> 00:05:40.280
Lost in the shuffle of a summer movie season
headlined by both Iron Man and The Dark Knight,

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:44.210
Hellboy II retained the same devoted appeal
of the original Hellboy, and remains one of

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:47.020
the shining beacons of the comic superhero
genre.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:52.910
"I just can't smile without yooouuuu."

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:54.830
The Devil's Backbone

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:59.710
del Toro's definitive ghost story, 2001's
The Devil's Backbone, is set during the last

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:03.861
days of the Spanish Civil War and follows
Carlos, a ten year old boy sheltered in a

00:06:03.861 --> 00:06:05.380
school for orphans.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:09.660
Confronted by the hostility of fellow orphan
Jaime, Carlos struggles to come to terms with

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:14.660
his situation until he uncovers the secret
of a ghost called Santi, who haunts the grounds.

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:17.050
What comes next is obviously unpleasant.

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:21.020
The Devil's Backbone is one of del Toro's
most personal films and excels by balancing

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.410
the
haunting scares with each character's emotional

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:24.410
honesty.

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:28.449
Both a creepy ghost story and powerful allegory
for the politics of the time, The Devil's

00:06:28.449 --> 00:06:33.080
Backbone is easily one of del Toro's best
movies to date and arguably forms the backbone

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.759
for everything he created after.

00:06:35.759 --> 00:06:37.160
The Shape of Water

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:42.640
Set in America circa 1962, del Toro's best
English-language film, The Shape of Water,

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:47.199
stars Sally Hawkins as Elisa, a lonely mute
woman who spends her days eating eggs and

00:06:47.199 --> 00:06:48.630
cleaning a government installation.

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:53.700
However, her life is changed forever when
she and her co-worker Zelda discover a humanoid

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.770
amphibian creature being abused by government
operatives.

00:06:56.770 --> 00:07:01.520
Playing the leading fish-man is del Toro's
frequently called-on character actor and contortionist

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:02.729
Doug Jones.

00:07:02.729 --> 00:07:06.450
Before the creature is killed, and with the
help of her best friend, Elisa plans to release

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:10.169
the creature out of captivity, but falls deeply
in love with him along the way.

00:07:10.169 --> 00:07:14.630
The Shape of Water successfully blends del
Toro's fantasy leanings and his romantic streak

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:19.130
in one cohesive, timeless story thanks to
his impressive production design and glorious

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:21.110
performances from the cast.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:24.860
Everything from its use of color to the unique
way it tackles communication helps The Shape

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:29.160
of Water transcend its bizarre subject matter
into an Oscar-winning opus.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:33.080
With its powerful underdog message, the film
is a true masterpiece and will continue to

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:36.030
be discussed by critics and audiences alike
for years to come.

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:38.030
"You know what I do, I don't know what I do.

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:40.150
I've been doing this for 25 years.

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:44.060
So whatever fruit I give, you can say it's
a pear or a pineapple.

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:46.360
Whatever it is, that's what I do."

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:47.360
Pan's Labyrinth

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:52.180
Just like The Devil's Backbone, Pan's Labyrinth
revisits the ramifications of the Spanish

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:53.180
Civil War.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:57.200
But here, both the horror and fantasy elements
are heightened further as the story follows

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:00.610
Ofelia's venture into a legendary underground
kingdom.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:04.889
Meanwhile, her stepfather plots to destroy
a nearby guerrilla uprising.

00:08:04.889 --> 00:08:09.490
Scoring an impressive 95 percent rating on
Rotten Tomatoes, Pan's Labyrinth is del Toro's

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:13.199
highest-rated movie and an undisputed triumph
of filmmaking.

00:08:13.199 --> 00:08:17.001
Despite being filmed in Spanish, the movie
became a surprise hit worldwide, the film

00:08:17.001 --> 00:08:21.270
won three Academy Awards for Best Art Direction,
Cinematography, and Makeup.

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:25.250
Pan's Labyrinth became del Toro's calling
card and captured the themes of innocence

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:28.240
and fantasy threaded throughout his entire
filmography.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:32.430
The film is both stunning and unnerving in
equal measure, and it's hard to imagine del

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:35.120
Toro ever creating another movie so wondrous.

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:36.120
Thanks for watching!

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:38.060
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:40.099
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

